 "history": {
  "discovered": "antiquity",
  "nameOrigin": "After the Roman god and planet Mercury, a name the alchemists gave to the quick, mobile liquid metal.",
  "symbolNote": "Hg comes from hydrargyrum, the Latinized form of the Greek for 'water-silver'.",
  "source": "pubchem",
  "note": "Found in Egyptian tombs of about 1500 BCE (Los Alamos)."
 },
 "prose": {
  "text": "Mercury is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature, melting at 234.32 K (−38.83 °C). Known since antiquity and refined from the red ore cinnabar, it once filled thermometers and barometers, but its vapour and compounds are toxic. It is so dense, 13.53 g/cm³, that a lump of lead floats on it. The liquidity is a relativistic effect: its two 6s electrons ([Xe] 4f¹⁴ 5d¹⁰ 6s², ground term ¹S₀) are drawn close to the heavy nucleus and held so tightly, giving the highest first ionisation energy of any metal (1007 kJ/mol), that mercury atoms bond only weakly to one another.",
  "sources": [
   "pubchem",
   "nist_gsie"
  ]
 }
}
